WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.801
Hi, I'm Pecan!

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:07.531
OpenMuse is a self-hostable personal agent that says it works with any harness.

00:00:07.531 --> 00:00:09.714
Here's how, and what stays behind.

00:00:10.975 --> 00:00:12.401
OpenMuse doesn't integrate harnesses.

00:00:12.401 --> 00:00:16.679
It speaks one protocol, and any agent that speaks it too drops

00:00:16.679 --> 00:00:18.106
into a single slot.

00:00:21.175 --> 00:00:23.660
The app talks to a small server.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.144
One environment variable decides which agent answers.

00:00:26.564 --> 00:00:31.052
By default, a built-in agent answers, and it brings twenty-nine tools:

00:00:31.052 --> 00:00:32.684
mail, browser, terminal, files.

00:00:33.104 --> 00:00:36.870
Switch it to AG-UI, and any harness can answer: one request in,

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.753
a stream of typed events back.

00:00:39.173 --> 00:00:42.789
But the tools live inside the agent it replaced, and the app

00:00:42.789 --> 00:00:44.898
draws eight cards by exact tool name.

00:00:44.898 --> 00:00:47.911
A swapped-in harness gets a chat pane, and no cards.

00:00:49.508 --> 00:00:52.948
Here's one of those cards, in the project's demo: a browser card

00:00:52.948 --> 00:00:55.528
with a live screenshot and a Take control button.

00:00:55.948 --> 00:00:59.571
The mail and browser tools here are real, on a fictional mailbox.

00:00:59.571 --> 00:01:01.383
The model's replies are a mock.

00:01:02.592 --> 00:01:04.686
The portability is real: five lines.

00:01:04.686 --> 00:01:09.223
The one harness adapter in the tree is disabled and connected to nothing.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.054
The agent's strongest tools only prepare.

00:01:14.054 --> 00:01:18.496
A prepared email waits for approval, and there is no tool to approve it.

00:01:18.916 --> 00:01:22.302
The proposal is hashed over its input, connection and target.

00:01:22.302 --> 00:01:26.028
Approving presents that hash from the app, so approval binds to

00:01:26.028 --> 00:01:27.382
exactly what you saw.

00:01:27.802 --> 00:01:32.485
One database update re-checks status, expiry and task state, so of two

00:01:32.485 --> 00:01:34.436
racing approvals, exactly one wins.

00:01:35.975 --> 00:01:37.872
You can replace who does the talking.

00:01:37.872 --> 00:01:39.769
You cannot replace who holds the keys.

00:01:42.692 --> 00:01:44.450
To recap: the talking is swappable.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:47.381
The tools, the cards and the approval gate are not.

00:01:47.801 --> 00:01:49.867
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:50.457
I'm Pecan.

00:01:50.457 --> 00:01:50.752
Bye!
